WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.379 --> 00:00:07.170
Most Easter eggs are discovered within a few
weeks or months of a video game's release.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:11.380
But once in awhile it takes a little longer
to find some of the secret goodies developers

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.309
bury within their products.

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:18.250
Let's put saving Hyrule on hold while we explore
some Easter eggs that were hidden a little

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:21.480
too well and took a whole lot longer to find.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.029
Halo 3: Happy Birthday Lauren

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:29.199
During Halo 3's creation, one of the developers
sneaked in a little message that took years

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:30.199
to find.

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:34.840
In fact, he actually had to reveal the secret
himself after seven years, since no one had

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:35.840
found it.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.480
Adrian Perez revealed in 2014 that he hid
a secret message in Halo 3's main loading

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:42.480
screen.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.280
The only way to get it was to set your console's
clock to December 25 (or play the game during

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.110
this actual day) and press in both thumbsticks
while the game is loading.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:55.420
"Happy Birthday Lauren" will appear on the
loading screen if done correctly.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.640
As cool as this little gift might be, it hardly
makes up for the fact that Lauren shares her

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:00.760
birthday with Christmas.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.340
That's the worst.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.429
The Legend of Zelda: A Link to the Past - Chris
Houlihan's room

00:01:06.429 --> 00:01:11.479
In 1990, Nintendo Power ran a contest that
would allow a fan to get their name included

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:16.020
in an unannounced SNES game, which turned
out to be A Link to the Past.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.799
It was revealed in the early 2000s that the
winner was a fan named Chris Houlihan.

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:25.270
In order to see his name, you'd have to glitch
Link into a secret room full of rupees and

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.560
a plaque that featured the lucky fan's name.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:32.729
But is having your name hidden within an impossible-to-find
glitch really that much of a prize?

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:37.109
Maybe Chris would've been better off taking
whatever was behind door number two...

00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:39.700
Final Fantasy IX: The Nero Brothers Quest

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:46.240
Despite being released in 2000, Final Fantasy
9's final quest was found a whopping 13 years

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.240
later.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.060
This quest had players encountering the Nero
brothers as they taught Zidane the finer points

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.170
of gambling.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:56.060
What made this secret so difficult to track
down was the fact that you had to find them

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:00.689
throughout multiple points in the game in
order to continue with the quest's progression.

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:05.170
And after all that work, you would have received
a Protect Ring as your reward, a solid, if

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:07.159
not particularly rare item.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:08.159
Yay?

00:02:08.159 --> 00:02:11.740
Batman: Arkham City - Calendar Man's Creepy
Message

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:16.390
In Batman: Arkham City, players can visit
the villain Calendar Man on different holidays

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.700
in the real world to listen to some unique
dialogue.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.270
After the Easter egg remained undiscovered
for three years, Rocksteady Studios intervened

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.640
and revealed how to discover this hidden bit
of dialogue.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:33.280
Here's the trick: if you set the date on your
PC or console to December 13, 2004 (the date

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.120
Rocksteady Studios was founded), you get a
message that references Batman: Arkham Knight,

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.610
the next game in the series...which, at the
time, hadn't even been announced yet:

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:48.530
"I was there at your beginning, and I will
be there at your end."

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:52.860
The irony, of course, is that Calendar Man
is obsessed with dates...but he'll never actually

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.530
score a date himself.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:55.890
Just look at him!

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.430
Splinter Cell: Double Agent - Seals!

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:03.950
Splinter Cell: Double Agent was released in
2006, but it wasn't until 2010 that players

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.300
learned, courtesy of one of the developers,
that they could find a secret mission to rescue

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.480
baby seals wearing party hats.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.130
They're just these cute little things with
names like Pepperoni and were beamed away

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.730
after being rescued.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.140
Because apparently they're aliens as well.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.250
Wave Race: Blue Storm - Sarcastic announcer

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.820
Wave Race: Blue Storm featured an announcer
who would provide color commentary whenever

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:28.570
players performed cool tricks on their jet
skis.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:33.730
In 2009, eight years after its GameCube release,
it was discovered that players could input

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:38.960
a code on the audio settings screen in order
to activate an alternative announcer...one

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.130
who was decidedly more sarcastic.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.280
As you raced, he would say things like:

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.970
"Supeeer"

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.660
"Greeaatttt"

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.110
"Oooo a turbo!"

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:51.110
It was...greeeat.

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:53.150
Mike Tyson's Punch-Out!!: Bald Bull's tell

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:57.819
Now here's an Easter egg that actually helped
players out in Mike Tyson's Punch-Out.

00:03:57.819 --> 00:04:03.710
The Easter egg involves the fight with Bald
Bull...and it wasn't discovered for 22 years.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:08.880
In 2009, former Nintendo CEO Satoru Iwata
revealed that there was an easier way to beat

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.440
Bald Bull that didn't require you to punch
him after his third hop in order to interrupt

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.660
his Bull Charge.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:18.590
All you had to do was look at the background
and try to spot an audience member who was

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:20.720
taking a picture with a flash camera.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.010
Once the flash went off, you could deliver
a body blow to the Bull to bring him down.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:28.450
That would've saved NES gamers a lot of misery
back in the '80s.

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:30.740
Well, better late than never, right?

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.370
After all the retro gamers out there learned
about Bald Bull's tell, a similar trick was

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.310
found for Piston Honda.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:43.500
On April 8, 2016, Reddit user and YouTuber
midwesternhousewives discovered a visual cue

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.130
that can help Little Mac beat Piston Honda
with ease.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:51.130
During your World Circuit rematch, Piston
Honda will go for a barrage of hits and will

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:52.850
backstep before charging.

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:57.030
Veteran gamers are well aware that you can
counter his delayed charge during this sequence

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.440
for a knockout, but you must have precise
timing.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.600
Similar to the Bald Bull trick, watch the
front of the crowd.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.250
On the left side, a bearded audience member
will nod/duck the exact moment you're supposed

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.020
to counter-punch Honda.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.460
This standout spectator also does the same
thing for Bald Bull's charge during your rematch

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.460
with him!

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:17.540
It's crazy to think that another Punch-Out!!

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:22.530
secret was hiding in plain sight until it
was discovered 28 years after the game's NES

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.530
debut.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:24.530
Thanks for watching!

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:28.000
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to see more
videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.020
And leave us a comment to let us know which
secrets you think should've made this list...

